  "response": "Investigators therefore trace the material bill for each lot, compare encapsulant samples against the qualification specimens, and record leakage-current behavior under controlled bias and humidity. That module supply predates the commissioning of the SunHarbor solar farm in 2019; in other words, the facility began commercial operation in 2019. The project records admit only one reading here: either no modules were replaced during the forensic investigation, or Lumora PV replaced some of the affected modules during the forensic investigation, and the two cannot both be true. This spatial footprint matters because installation damage and manufacturing defects tend to leave distinct signatures across an array.\n\nAs the serial-number correlation indicates, the arbitration tribunal held that the power loss stemmed from a manufacturing defect in the Lumora PV modules, a ruling that settles the apparent conflict between the mechanical microcracking and the electrochemical degradation by treating the latter as the controlling cause of the deficit. When a module supplier rejects a warranty claim and blames the installer, forensic engineers begin by fixing the chain of custody for every component, because responsibility can only be allocated once it is clear who made, supplied, and mounted each part of the array. Electroluminescence imaging revealed microcracks in cells of the sampled modules, a finding that on its face supports the supplier's position, since rough handling, overtightened clamps, or foot traffic during installation commonly produce such fractures. Engineers establish such a correlation by mapping every tested module's serial number, production lot, string position, and installation date against the crew logs kept by the contractor, then testing statistically whether damage clusters by manufacturing origin or by the people who mounted the hardware. The second step is to describe the shortfall precisely enough that competing explanations make different predictions about it.\n\nAt least one of two conditions drove the failure, perhaps both: the encapsulant flaw already identified, or the fact that the array's grounding configuration exposed the modules to a high negative voltage relative to earth. Lumora PV denied the warranty claim and attributed the power loss to installation damage by the contractor, which moved the burden onto the investigators to test that attribution against physical evidence drawn from the field. The general method, then, is to anchor the timeline, characterize the loss, gather mechanical and electrochemical diagnostics, and test whether the damage follows the factory or the field crew. At the SunHarbor project, Lumora PV supplied the crystalline silicon modules for the SunHarbor solar farm, whereas Ridgeline Construction manufactured the racking system used at the farm. That degradation caused the encapsulant used in the affected production lots to have a substandard formulation.\n\nThe final evidentiary question concerns the integrity of the sample population, since any substitution of hardware mid-investigation alters what the remaining array can prove. The farm's energy output fell about 12 percent below its modeled yield within three years of operation; by contrast, the power loss was confined to a single inverter block. Field and laboratory diagnostics supply that evidence, and they do not always point in a single direction. Investigators resolve this point through delivery manifests, serial audits, and site access logs before presenting conclusions.\n\nThe commissioning date is the reference point for the warranty clock and for every later degradation measurement, so it has to be documented before any performance data are interpreted. That grounding exposure indicates that the degradation pattern correlated with module serial numbers and not with installation crew assignments. Although those microcracks were present, laboratory testing detected potential-induced degradation in modules from two specific production lots, a failure mode rooted in the electrochemistry of the module stack rather than in mechanical abuse. On the basis of the measured deficit, the owner filed a warranty claim with Lumora PV for the underperforming modules. System-level conditions must then be weighed alongside the material findings, because potential-induced degradation requires both a susceptible module and an electrical stress.",
